section.para:
			The scorer accepts text and spans to exclude. A dictionary predicate accepts
			lowercase words. Recognized words are omitted from the results. Otherwise, a
			token with no structural or binary-content signal is reported as misspelled
			when a single OCR substitution produces a dictionary word:

Set<String> words = Set.of("modern", "times");
NoiseScorer scorer = new StructuralNoiseScorer(words::contains);

String text = "rnodern tirnes and zxkcvbnmsdfg";
for (NoiseSpan noise : scorer.score(text, List.of())) {
    System.out.println(noise.severity() + " " + noise.span());
}

			The substitution rn to m produces the dictionary
			words modern times. Consecutive results separated by whitespace
			are merged:

misspelled [0..14)
gibberish [19..31)

			Without a dictionary, this example reports only the gibberish span.

			NoiseAnnotator provides opennlp:noise. In its
			default mode it requires opennlp:assets and excludes those spans
			from scoring. Run the asset annotator first:

String text = "The scan contains zxkcvbnmsdfg here.";
Document document = new AssetAnnotator().annotate(Document.of(text));
document = new NoiseAnnotator().annotate(document);

List<Annotation<NoiseSpan>> noise = document.get(NoiseAnnotator.NOISE);

			This produces a gibberish annotation for zxkcvbnmsdfg. The asset
			layer may be empty, but it must be present. To scan without asset detection,
			select standalone mode:

Document document = new NoiseAnnotator(new StructuralNoiseScorer(), false)
    .annotate(Document.of("The scan contains zxkcvbnmsdfg here."));
List<Annotation<NoiseSpan>> noise = document.get(NoiseAnnotator.NOISE);
